    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'hi' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi, Max.' Sasha links 'hi' directly with 'Max' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Как дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How are you?' Sasha combines 'how' with 'things' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывало и лучше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I've been better.' Notice how 'to happen' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'better'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Всё ещё переживаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you still worrying?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'to worry'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, no.' Max links 'yes' directly with 'no' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С Викой всё в прошлом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'With Vika everything is in the past.' Max links 'with' directly with 'Vika' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Ты уверен?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you sure?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'you' straight to 'sure'. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'of course' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Ну ладно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, okay.' Sasha links 'oh' directly with 'all right' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Мне пора работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It's time for me to work.' Listen for the word meaning 'to me' right alongside 'it's time' in this line. And Max jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Увидимся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'See you.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'will see each other' turns the action back onto the subject. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
